#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497461} :activity/id #uuid "cbb9bff8-0fb7-50be-adc5-7623e34f2040" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049575 :topic/id #uuid "01533816-c819-5cab-8910-7cf6afa63383" :topic/math-academy-id 6685} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497440 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308376 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e50e8ab-e411-586f-a3d4-dc2e4fcd7cb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466065} {:answer/id #uuid "fc8afd2e-ae6c-570d-a8ff-76907c67d059" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466066}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186466066} :answer-field/id #uuid "62b9e8dd-90d0-51c7-be44-1b2eb0a32755" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466876} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c56cefe7-8454-534e-b476-cc7a1d062420" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466067} {:answer/id #uuid "0cfa897d-e2a7-5a0a-9895-d4e337ada051" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466068}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186466067} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1061fc5-5342-526c-88c1-d457329519a0" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466877} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e25c152c-cde8-5b9b-a39d-1c434bef3856" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466069} {:answer/id #uuid "2db53487-34f5-58b2-a4f4-a19aaa44c64f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466070}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186466069} :answer-field/id #uuid "c682ebed-3244-5b41-a799-d3bf75036c38" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466878} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f3833cd4-b47d-541f-9a24-3a41b77197eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466071} {:answer/id #uuid "5c5fdda5-251a-5d00-8098-412f1be153f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466072}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186466072} :answer-field/id #uuid "030e73ec-670f-572f-9967-01c8e343d51e" :answer-field/key "IV" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466879}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "30787f50-63d0-5577-9588-239251d45d11" :question/math-academy-id "q-333092" :question/problem "For which of the following values is Euler's method stable, given that $\\lambda $ is the parameter of the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),y(0)=1,x\\ge 0,\\lambda >0,$ and step size $Δx?$  \n- **I.** $\\lambda Δx=0.4$\n- **II.** $\\lambda Δx=2.0$\n- **III.** $\\lambda Δx=2.4$\n- **IV.** $\\lambda Δx=1.2$\n\n*Hint: The stability function for Euler's method is given by* $R(\\lambda Δx)=1-\\lambda Δx.$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nFor stability, we require that the following condition holds:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(\\lambda Δx)| & <1 \\\\ |1-\\lambda Δx| & <1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this gives\n\n$$\n0<\\lambda Δx<2.\n$$\n\nThis means that Euler's method is stable when applied to the test equation for values in the region $0<\\lambda Δx<2.$ Of the given values:\n\n- $\\lambda Δx=0.4$ falls *within* the given region,\n\n- $\\lambda Δx=2.0$ falls *outside* of the given region,\n\n- $\\lambda Δx=2.4$ falls *outside* the given region, and\n\n- $\\lambda Δx=1.2$ falls *within* the given region.\n\nHence, Euler's method is stable for $\\lambda Δx=0.4$ and $\\lambda Δx=1.2$ only.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and IV only\"."} :step/id #uuid "61a72ca7-449a-52f3-bf8e-93853046baf7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497468}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497461 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229636 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a567c91c-11d7-5940-8adc-f09cd2d0fddf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462263} {:answer/id #uuid "31e3f346-f2a6-5edb-91d3-5031fb7f980a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462264}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186462264} :answer-field/id #uuid "1fcd9ee7-351d-5544-b31e-d5339038bd16" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462885} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ffee5da-bd54-550d-bf31-632cd5a50672" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462265} {:answer/id #uuid "76edda26-bda9-5fec-afe7-be2d775dfcb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462266}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186462266} :answer-field/id #uuid "d6255aa2-753f-5c9f-ae01-074e1e981e62" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462886} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "556da2f8-4988-5c21-afbc-f09ab1f4e7d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462267} {:answer/id #uuid "5ab302c1-916a-5999-ad86-77bdb3a80d80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462268}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186462268} :answer-field/id #uuid "79c582a6-0da6-593d-81ce-223c901e263a" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462887} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c10f69f1-f4a6-5042-b2b9-344a17db3922" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462269} {:answer/id #uuid "dbaa2383-6061-5021-8da6-142a8d04e77d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462270}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186462269} :answer-field/id #uuid "7225a2f4-1737-5b07-bd07-4b0019b5b353" :answer-field/key "IV" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462888}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3afc7fc5-d735-57e5-883a-73d956edd87b" :question/math-academy-id "q-332533" :question/problem "For which of the following values is Euler's method stable, given that $\\lambda $ is the parameter of the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),y(0)=1,x\\ge 0,\\lambda >0,$ and step size $Δx?$\n- **I.** $\\lambda Δx=0.67$\n- **II.** $\\lambda Δx=1.20$\n- **III.** $\\lambda Δx=1.80$\n- **IV.** $\\lambda Δx=2.25$\n\n*Hint: The stability function for Euler's method is given by* $R(\\lambda Δx)=1-\\lambda Δx.$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nFor stability, we require that the following condition holds:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(\\lambda Δx)| & <1 \\\\ |1-\\lambda Δx| & <1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this gives\n\n$$\n0<\\lambda Δx<2.\n$$\n\nThis means that Euler's method is stable when applied to the test equation for values in the region $0<\\lambda Δx<2.$ Of the given values:\n\n- $\\lambda Δx=0.67$ falls *within* the given region,\n\n- $\\lambda Δx=1.20$ falls *within* the given region,\n\n- $\\lambda Δx=1.80$ falls *within* the given region, and\n\n- $\\lambda Δx=2.25$ falls *outside* the given region.\n\nHence, Euler's method is stable for the values $\\lambda Δx=0.67,$ $\\lambda Δx=1.20,$ and $\\lambda Δx=1.80.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only\"."} :step/id #uuid "a3d07d02-d1d6-58a1-be52-3ef85dfeafe1" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497467}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497467 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229646 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c495fcbe-46e4-5570-a5d1-28127f914a1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228101} {:answer/id #uuid "db63787a-f64d-520f-b951-801e1f9c89e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, III, and IV" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228102} {:answer/id #uuid "f9029063-2276-5c5e-aaae-5419b3c1391e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228103} {:answer/id #uuid "62130c11-c8cc-5bfd-aa1b-21009a57c496" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228104} {:answer/id #uuid "75075a4d-6bc9-5c33-a9f0-607303832709" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228105}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228104} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b55e392-d0cf-5935-be82-e8780c5e7cc5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228100}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e90b20b3-e8ec-5eff-9813-0425b7acc64b" :question/math-academy-id "q-332744" :question/problem "For which of the following values is the Runge-Kutta Method (RK4) stable, given that $\\lambda $ is the parameter of the test equation?\n\nI. $\\lambda Δx=0.4$\n\nII. $\\lambda Δx=1$\n\nIII. $\\lambda Δx=2$\n\nIV. $\\lambda Δx=3$\n\n*Hint: The stability function $R$ of the RK4 method is given by*\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δ)=1-\\lambda Δx+\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{3}}{6}+\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{4}}{24}.\n$$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda Δx)$ is referred to as the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda Δx)|<1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nThe stability function of the RK4 Method is\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δx)=1-\\lambda Δx+\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{3}}{6}+\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{4}}{24}.\n$$\n\nLet's check the stability of the RK4 Method for each of the given values in turn.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=0.4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(0.4)| & =|1-0.4+\\frac{(0.4)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(0.4)^{3}}{6}+\\frac{(0.4)^{4}}{24}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{419}{625}| \\\\  & =0.670\\,4<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the RK4 Method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(1)| & =|1-1+\\frac{(1)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(1)^{3}}{6}+\\frac{(1)^{4}}{24}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{3}{8}| \\\\  & =0.375<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the RK4 Method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(2)| & =|1-2+\\frac{(2)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(2)^{3}}{6}+\\frac{(2)^{4}}{24}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1}{3}| \\\\  & =0.333…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the RK4 Method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(3)| & =|1-3+\\frac{(3)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(3)^{3}}{6}+\\frac{(3)^{4}}{24}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{11}{8}| \\\\  & =1.375<1.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *outside* the stability region, the RK4 Method is unstable.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only\".\n\nNote that the stability region of the RK4 Method is $0<\\lambda Δx<2.785…,$ so it is not absolutely stable."} :step/id #uuid "ac815580-094f-5483-a534-42ce5406ed84" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497440}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497468 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497342 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c86d4ff-e60d-5ed0-9b7e-51fd840bb413" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496789} {:answer/id #uuid "469a895b-ff19-5c22-9203-6eb75d050f16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496812} {:answer/id #uuid "501f49f4-5d2c-516d-b744-0038bbab282c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496833} {:answer/id #uuid "525fd160-0089-59c0-a76b-1ab57865318a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496836} {:answer/id #uuid "80c052ba-48ac-56d0-8ff1-de202eea8817" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496923}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496923} :answer-field/id #uuid "a124cc3f-17e0-53b5-aed5-2049adc80c6e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497294}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "25d64691-5b6a-542f-814d-ce1c552ea7d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-333544" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true when testing the stability of the implicit Euler method on the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),y(0)=1,x\\ge 0,\\lambda =4,$ and step size $Δx?$  \n\nI. The stability function is $R(4Δx)=\\frac{1}{1+4Δx}.$\n\nII. The stability region is $|\\frac{1}{1+4Δx}|<1.$\n\nIII. The method is absolutely stable.\n\nIV. The method is **unstable** for step size $Δx=0.1.$\n\n*Hint:* The stability function $R$ is given by ${y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n}.$" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method ${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda Δx)$ is the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda Δx)|<1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nA method that is stable for all possible values $\\lambda Δx>0$ is called absolutely stable.\n\nNow, let's check the stability of the implicit Euler method by applying it to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-4y,\\,y(0)=1.\n$$\n\nFirst, for an initial value problem ${y}^{′}=f(x,y),y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},$ the implicit Euler method is given by the update formula\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+f({x}_{n+1},{y}_{n+1})\\cdot Δx.\n$$\n\nWe now examine our statements:\n\n- Applying the implicit Euler method to the test equation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+(-4{y}_{n+1})\\cdot Δx \\\\ {y}_{n+1}+4{y}_{n+1}\\cdot Δx & ={y}_{n} \\\\ {y}_{n+1}(1+4Δx) & ={y}_{n} \\\\ {y}_{n+1} & =\\frac{1}{1+4Δx}\\cdot {y}_{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the stability function is \n\n$$\nR(4Δx)=\\frac{1}{1+4Δx}.\n$$\n\nHence, statement I is true.\n\n- The method is stable when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(4Δx)| & <1 \\\\ |\\frac{1}{1+4Δx}| & <1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${y}_{n}$ converges to $0$ as $n\\to \\infty $ if and only if\n$|\\frac{1}{1+4Δx}|<1.$\nHence, statement II is true.\n\n- Now, since $1+4Δx>1$ for any positive step size $Δx,$ we have\n\n$$\n|\\frac{1}{1+4Δx}|<1\n$$\n\nfor all $Δx>0.$ So, the implicit Euler method is absolutely stable, and statement III is true.\n\n- Finally, because the implicit Euler method is absolutely stable, it is stable for step size $Δx=0.1.$ Hence, statement IV is false.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only\"."} :step/id #uuid "ad7e16c0-6c4a-571d-8f1d-bcb5e05cd25b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497481}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497481 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497371 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c4e0507-df7f-5aa5-98f1-856c82ebe79f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496756} {:answer/id #uuid "748cd873-307f-5e75-9f8f-77a3d766273d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496905} {:answer/id #uuid "78dba489-7ba7-5d7e-a78f-ab1fdacb56a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496913} {:answer/id #uuid "d6203cbd-f862-5bd5-a4a5-da7556b09cdd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, III, and IV" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497105} {:answer/id #uuid "f4d3c51b-b3a8-5879-b596-2a32d0b50eda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497180}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497105} :answer-field/id #uuid "f48506ec-17d9-5b93-9a61-0014927029a1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497312}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "74bb58bc-5799-594f-9fe1-c2f6de777ad6" :question/math-academy-id "q-334247" :question/problem "For which of the following values is the implicit Euler method stable, given that $\\lambda $ is the parameter of the test equation?\n\nI. $\\lambda Δx=0.4$\n\nII. $\\lambda Δx=1.3$\n\nIII. $\\lambda Δx=4$\n\nIV. $\\lambda Δx=0.05$\n\n*Hint: The stability function $R$ of the implicit Euler method is given by*\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δx)=\\frac{1}{1+\\lambda Δx}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda Δx)$ is referred to as the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda Δx)|<1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nThe stability function of the implicit Euler method is\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δx)=\\frac{1}{1+\\lambda Δx}.\n$$\n\nLet's check the stability of the Implicit Euler Method for each of the given values in turn.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=0.4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(0.4)| & =|\\frac{1}{1+0.4}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1}{1.4}| \\\\  & =0.7142…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the implicit Euler method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=1.3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(1.3)| & =|\\frac{1}{1+1.3}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1}{2.3}| \\\\  & =0.4347…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the implicit Euler method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(4)| & =|\\frac{1}{1+4}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1}{5}| \\\\  & =0.2<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the implicit Euler method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=0.05,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(0.05)| & =|\\frac{1}{1+0.05}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1}{1.05}| \\\\  & =0.9523<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the implicit Euler method is stable.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, III, and IV\".\n\nNote that, since the implicit Euler method is absolutely stable, it is stable for all possible values of $\\lambda Δx.$"} :step/id #uuid "e7a32cad-b05c-53f3-8a55-0a0ff5e0e6be"}] :activity/title "Stability of Numerical Methods" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496657}]}
